
(verb
  :name "call-with-current-continuation" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(call-with-current-continuation proc) -> value"
  :summary "R7RS §6.10 — call proc with an escape procedure; calling that escape procedure makes the whole call-with-current-continuation form return the given value immediately."
  :explanation "The long name for `call/cc`, and in Motoi the two are literally the same procedure — (eq? call/cc call-with-current-continuation) is #t. It hands your procedure one argument, conventionally named k or `return` or `escape`, which is a way OUT. Call it with a value and the entire (call-with-current-continuation …) expression finishes right there and yields that value; whatever was still to be done inside proc is abandoned, however deep in a loop or a recursion it was. Don't call it and proc's ordinary return value is the answer instead. What you get here is an ESCAPE, and only an escape. Full Scheme continuations can also be re-entered — you save k, let the form return, then call k later to go back and run the rest of that computation again, which is how generators and coroutines are built. Motoi does not do that (decision-004): the continuation is a one-way exit that is only live while proc is still running, and calling a saved one afterwards fails with `error: undefined`. Everything R7RS uses call/cc for that is really early-return — search loops, bail-outs, non-local exit from nested traversal — works exactly as written. Everything that resumes does not."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (k) (+ 1 2)))" :note "3 — k was never called, so proc's ordinary return value is the answer.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (k) (k 42) 99))" :note "42 — calling k ends the form immediately; the 99 after it is never reached.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (return) (for-each (lambda (x) (if (even? x) (return x))) (list 1 3 8 5)) 'none))" :note "8 — early return out of the middle of a for-each, which for-each has no other way to offer.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (outer) (+ 100 (call-with-current-continuation (lambda (inner) (outer 7))))))" :note "7 — the escape jumps past the pending (+ 100 …) as well; it exits to ITS OWN form, not to the nearest one.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (find-first pred xs)\n  (call/cc (lambda (return)\n    (for-each (lambda (x) (if (pred x) (return x))) xs)\n    'not-found)))\n(display (list (find-first even? (list 1 3 8 5)) (find-first even? (list 1 3 5))))\n(newline)" :note "(8 not-found) — the escape supplies the found value and the fall-through supplies the not-found value.")
  )
  :caveats ("Motoi's continuations are ESCAPE-ONLY (decision-004). A continuation is live only while proc is still running." "Calling a SAVED continuation after its call-with-current-continuation form has already returned fails with `error: undefined` and stops the program. Generators, coroutines and backtracking that re-enter a continuation cannot be written this way here." "call/cc is the same procedure, not a wrapper: (eq? call/cc call-with-current-continuation) is #t." "The escape procedure accepts any number of arguments. (k 1 2) returns two values; (k) returns nothing, so (list (call/cc (lambda (k) (k)))) is ()." "Escaping past a dynamic-wind DOES run that wind's after-thunk, so cleanup still happens on the way out." "An escape crossing a with-exception-handler is not a raise and the handler does not see it.")
  :usecases ("Early return from the middle of for-each, map or a fold that has no break." "Bailing out of a nested traversal the moment an answer is found." "Short-circuiting a product or a sum when a zero or an absorbing element appears." "Turning a callback-style search into a procedure that simply returns its answer.")
  :related ("call/cc" "dynamic-wind" "with-exception-handler" "raise" "values" "for-each")
  :learn (:concept "A continuation is 'what happens next' turned into a procedure you can call; calling it skips straight to the end of the form that gave it to you." :prerequisites ("lambda" "for-each") :progression "First write a proc that ignores k and see the ordinary value come back. Then call k early and watch the rest be skipped. Then put the call inside a for-each, which is where the escape earns its keep. Stop before saving k for later — that is the part Motoi does not do.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.10; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:916-930, escape-only per decision-004; measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (find-first pred xs)\n  (call/cc (lambda (return)\n    (for-each (lambda (x) (if (pred x) (return x))) xs)\n    'not-found)))\n(display (list (find-first even? (list 1 3 8 5)) (find-first even? (list 1 3 5))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (product xs)\n  (call/cc (lambda (return)\n    (fold (lambda (x acc) (if (zero? x) (return 0) (* x acc))) 1 xs))))\n(display (list (product (list 2 3 4)) (product (list 2 0 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-div-all xs d)\n  (call/cc (lambda (bail) (map (lambda (x) (if (zero? d) (bail 'no-divisor) (/ x d))) xs))))\n(display (list (safe-div-all (list 4 8) 2) (safe-div-all (list 4 8) 0)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A traversal must stop the instant it has its answer, but for-each and map have no break. Candidates: keep a found? flag and let the loop run to the end anyway (correct answer, wasted work, and the flag has to be checked in two places), rewrite the walk as a hand-written recursion that can return early (works, but you have re-implemented for-each), or hand the loop an escape procedure."
     :explanation "eng: the search returns the moment it finds an 8 and never looks at the 5. math: a product short-circuits at the absorbing element, which is not an optimisation but the definition — anything times zero is zero, so the rest of the factors cannot matter. cs: the bail-out replaces the whole map result with one value, not one element of it."
     :meta "Reach for call-with-current-continuation and NOT a found? flag when the loop cannot stop. A flag lets the loop finish and then discards the work; the escape does not let it finish. Use raise instead when the early exit is a FAILURE — a handler can be caught and reported, and an escape cannot.")
    (:eng "Stopping early is a correctness property as much as a speed one when the remaining elements are expensive to touch." :math "Short-circuit at an absorbing element: 0 for multiplication, #t for or, #f for and." :cs "Same shape as break in an imperative loop, or a return from inside a nested block — expressed as a value rather than as syntax."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Being handed a door out of where you are, so you can leave from the middle instead of walking to the end."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (k) (k 42) 99))")
       (:domain "a fire door in a long corridor" :code "(display (call/cc (lambda (done) (for-each (lambda (x) (if (> x 100) (done x))) (list 5 50 500 5000)) 'never-exceeded)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a proof that stops the moment it finds a counterexample" :code "(display (call/cc (lambda (escape) (+ 1 (call/cc (lambda (inner) (escape 'jumped-past-the-plus)))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A fire door is not on the route; it exists so nobody has to finish the route. A counterexample ends the search for a proof immediately — no remaining case can change the verdict. In all three the exit is decided at the point of discovery, and everything downstream of that point is simply not done. The third rung is the sharp one: the escape leaves past a pending (+ 1 …), so 'downstream' means the enclosing arithmetic too, not just the rest of the loop."
     :meta "The shape is exit-from-the-middle, and its cost is always the same: whoever reads the code cannot see the exit at the exit point, only at the door. That is why the escape procedure deserves a name like `return`, `done` or `bail` and never `k` in code that other people will read.")
    (:eng "Name the escape after what leaving means, because the call site is the only clue a reader gets." :math "One counterexample settles a universal claim; the search does not have to be exhaustive to be conclusive." :cs "Non-local exit. The stack between the escape and its call/cc is discarded, which is why re-entry needs machinery Motoi does not have."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list 'not-called (call/cc (lambda (k) 'plain)) 'called (call/cc (lambda (k) (k 'early) 'late))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (call/cc (lambda (outer) (+ 100 (call/cc (lambda (inner) (outer 7)))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list (eq? call/cc call-with-current-continuation) (call/cc (lambda (k) (k 1 2)))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "The form yields proc's return value when the escape is not called, and the escape's argument when it is — and in the second case no pending computation between the escape and its own call/cc form is performed."
     :invariant "For all proc. call/cc(proc) = proc(k) if k is never applied; = v if k is applied to v, with every pending frame between the application and this call/cc discarded"
     :meta "The math program is the whole discarding claim in one line: the escape belongs to the OUTER form, so calling it skips the inner form and the (+ 100 …) alike, and 7 comes out rather than 107. What would falsify it: an answer of 107, which is what an implementation that merely returned a value from the inner lambda would produce. The third program pins the aliasing and shows the escape is variadic."
    )
    (:eng "Two arms, one line, no setup — this is the cheapest complete test of an escape." :math "107 versus 7 is the observable difference between returning a value and abandoning a frame." :cs "eq? on the two names proves they are one binding; (k 1 2) shows the escape carries the same multiple-value convention as an ordinary return."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (with-early-exit body) (call/cc body))\n(define (scan grid target)\n  (with-early-exit (lambda (found)\n    (for-each (lambda (row) (for-each (lambda (cell) (if (eq? cell target) (found 'hit))) row)) grid)\n    'miss)))\n(display (list (scan (list (list 'a 'b) (list 'c 'x)) 'x) (scan (list (list 'a 'b)) 'x)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (any-satisfying pred xs)\n  (call/cc (lambda (yes) (for-each (lambda (x) (if (pred x) (yes x))) xs) #f)))\n(display (list (any-satisfying negative? (list 3 -2 5)) (any-satisfying negative? (list 3 2 5))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (guard-both thunk)\n  (call/cc (lambda (escape) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (escape (list 'raised (error-object-message e)))) thunk))))\n(display (list (guard-both (lambda () 'ok)) (guard-both (lambda () (error \"boom\")))))\n(newline)"
     :base "call-with-current-continuation hands out a one-way exit from its own form."
     :emergent "eng: a named with-early-exit wrapper, and a two-level grid scan that leaves both loops at once — the inner for-each has no idea it is being abandoned. math: `any`, rebuilt from scratch, returning the witness rather than #t. cs: an escape wrapped around a handler, so a raise anywhere inside the thunk becomes a value at a single known place."
     :meta "The composition worth keeping is the last one. call/cc gives you WHERE to land and with-exception-handler gives you WHAT went wrong; together they are a guard whose recovery point is chosen by the caller rather than by whoever wrote the raise. That is the whole design of guard in Schemes that have it, assembled here out of two smaller verbs — and it is the closest Motoi's escape-only continuations get to the full article."
    )
    (:eng "Escaping two nested loops at once is the case where a flag would need to be checked twice and forgotten once." :math "Returning the witness instead of #t costs nothing and gives the caller something to work with." :cs "Escape plus handler is a guard. Building it from parts is worth doing once so you can see there is no magic in it."))
)

(verb
  :name "dynamic-wind" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(dynamic-wind before thunk after) -> value"
  :summary "R7RS §6.10 — run before, then thunk, then after; the value is thunk's, and after runs even if thunk raises or escapes."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"1\")) (lambda () (display \"2\") 'value) (lambda () (display \"3\")))" :note "123value — the three thunks run in order, then the top level prints thunk's value.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(dynamic-wind (lambda () 'ignored-before) (lambda () 'the-value) (lambda () 'ignored-after))" :note "the-value — only the middle thunk's result is the value of the form; before and after are run for effect and their results are dropped.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'recovered) (lambda () (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[open]\")) (lambda () (error \"mid-computation\")) (lambda () (display \"[close]\")))))" :note "[open][close]recovered — the thunk raised, [close] still ran, and only then did the handler get its turn.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(call/cc (lambda (k) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[in]\")) (lambda () (k 'escaped)) (lambda () (display \"[out]\")))))" :note "[in][out]escaped — escaping through the wind with a continuation still runs the after-thunk on the way out.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (with-restore get set new thunk)\n  (let ((old (get))) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (set new)) thunk (lambda () (set old)))))\n(define colour 'black)\n(display (list (with-restore (lambda () colour) (lambda (v) (set! colour v)) 'red (lambda () colour)) 'after colour))\n(newline)" :note "(red after black) — inside the wind the colour is red, and afterwards it is exactly what it was before.")
  )
  :caveats ("Motoi has no re-entrant continuations (decision-004), so the before-thunk never runs a second time. In a full Scheme, re-entering a saved continuation would re-run before; here that situation cannot arise." "The value of the form is thunk's value. Whatever before and after return is discarded." "after runs on the normal path, on a raise, and on a call/cc escape. It is implemented as a JS finally, so there is no path out of the thunk that skips it." "after runs BEFORE the enclosing handler, not after it — the cleanup output appears first and the handler's value comes second." "A raise inside the after-thunk itself will replace the condition already in flight. Keep cleanup code dull." "Nesting winds nest their cleanups: the inner after runs before the outer after.")
  :usecases ("Restoring a variable, a drawing mode or a colour to its previous value whatever happens in between." "Releasing something that was acquired — a lock name, a slot in a list, a depth counter." "Bracketing a region with matched enter/leave log lines that cannot get out of step." "Guaranteeing an unwind step when the body may raise or escape.")
  :related ("call-with-current-continuation" "call/cc" "with-exception-handler" "parameterize" "make-parameter" "raise")
  :learn (:concept "Some pairs of actions must always happen together — open and close, acquire and release — and dynamic-wind is how you say so once instead of at every exit." :prerequisites ("lambda" "set!" "with-exception-handler") :progression "Run the three thunks and watch the order. Then make the middle one raise and see the after still run. Then use it to save and restore a variable, which is the shape almost every real use has.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.10; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:895 (try/finally; re-entry unsupported per decision-004); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define log '())\n(define (with-mode m thunk)\n  (dynamic-wind (lambda () (set! log (cons (list 'enter m) log)))\n                thunk\n                (lambda () (set! log (cons (list 'leave m) log)))))\n(display (list (with-mode 'draw (lambda () 'painted)) (reverse log)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'recovered) (lambda () (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[open]\")) (lambda () (error \"mid-computation\")) (lambda () (display \"[close]\"))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define depth 0)\n(define (traced thunk) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (set! depth (+ depth 1))) thunk (lambda () (set! depth (- depth 1)))))\n(display (list (traced (lambda () (traced (lambda () depth)))) 'after depth))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart switches drawing mode, paints, and must switch back — and the paint step can fail. Candidates: set, paint, unset in sequence (the unset is skipped on any failure, and the next frame draws in the wrong mode forever), wrap the paint in a handler and unset in both arms (correct, and the unset is now written twice and will be edited once), or bracket the whole thing with dynamic-wind."
     :explanation "eng: enter and leave are logged from one place, so they cannot get out of step. math: the raise happens mid-computation and [close] still appears — before the handler's value, which is the ordering to remember. cs: a depth counter that goes 1, 2, and back to 0 through nesting, because each after undoes exactly its own before."
     :meta "Reach for dynamic-wind and NOT a handler with duplicated cleanup. A handler catches; a wind unwinds. The difference matters most when there are several ways out — normal return, raise, escape — because the wind covers all of them and a hand-written cleanup covers the ones you remembered.")
    (:eng "Paired log lines that cannot desynchronise are worth more in an incident than any amount of extra logging." :math "The counter returns to its starting value, which is the invariant the whole construction exists to keep." :cs "try/finally, RAII, defer — the same idea under three names, and this is the Scheme spelling."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Two actions that must be paired, written down once, so that no way of leaving the middle can skip the second one."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"1\")) (lambda () (display \"2\") 'value) (lambda () (display \"3\")))")
       (:domain "an airlock you cannot walk out of without cycling" :code "(display (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"A\")) (lambda () (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"B\")) (lambda () 'inner) (lambda () (display \"C\")))) (lambda () (display \"D\"))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "matched brackets in a well-formed expression" :code "(display (call/cc (lambda (k) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[in]\")) (lambda () (k 'escaped)) (lambda () (display \"[out]\"))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "An airlock cycles on the way out whether you leave calmly or in a hurry — the leaving is what triggers it, not your intention. Matched brackets nest and never cross: an inner pair closes before the outer one, which is exactly ABCD in the second rung. The third shows the airlock working under the hurried case: a continuation escape is not a normal return and [out] is printed anyway."
     :meta "Well-nestedness is the property all three share, and it is what makes the guarantee composable. If cleanups could cross, two correct winds could combine into an incorrect one — so the rule that the inner after precedes the outer after is not a detail, it is the reason you can nest these at all."
    )
    (:eng "Cleanup that triggers on leaving rather than on intending to leave is the only kind that survives a surprise." :math "Properly nested delimiters — the same structure that makes an expression parseable makes an unwind sound." :cs "LIFO unwinding. The stack discipline of the winds mirrors the stack discipline of the calls."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (dynamic-wind (lambda () 'ignored-before) (lambda () 'the-value) (lambda () 'ignored-after)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'outer-caught) (lambda () (dynamic-wind (lambda () 'a) (lambda () (raise 'x)) (lambda () (display \"[cleanup ran]\"))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (call/cc (lambda (k) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (display \"[in]\")) (lambda () (k 'escaped) 'unreachable) (lambda () (display \"[out]\"))))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "The after-thunk runs on every exit from the body — normal return, raise, and continuation escape — and the value of the form is always the body's, never before's or after's."
     :invariant "For all before b, thunk t, after a. dynamic-wind(b,t,a) evaluates b then t; a is evaluated exactly once regardless of how t terminates; and the result is t's result when t returns normally"
     :meta "Three exits, three programs: the first returns, the second raises, the third escapes, and [cleanup ran] and [out] prove the after fired in the two abnormal cases. What would falsify it: an exit path that printed nothing — which is what a naive before/body/after sequence without a finally would do on the raise. The second program also pins the ORDERING: the cleanup output appears before the handler's value, so cleanup precedes recovery."
    )
    (:eng "Exactly-once is the claim that matters; twice is as much a bug as never." :math "The guarantee is universally quantified over how the body ends, which is why it can be relied on in code that has not been written yet." :cs "Implemented as a JS finally at lib/base/r7rs-small.js:899, so the guarantee is the host's, not a convention."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define held '())\n(define (borrow name thunk)\n  (dynamic-wind (lambda () (set! held (cons name held))) thunk (lambda () (set! held (cdr held)))))\n(display (list (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'failed) (lambda () (borrow 'brush (lambda () (error \"slipped\"))))) 'still-held held))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (timed thunk) (dynamic-wind (lambda () 'start) thunk (lambda () 'stop)))\n(display (map (lambda (n) (timed (lambda () (* n n)))) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (with-restore get set new thunk)\n  (let ((old (get))) (dynamic-wind (lambda () (set new)) thunk (lambda () (set old)))))\n(define colour 'black)\n(display (list (with-restore (lambda () colour) (lambda (v) (set! colour v)) 'red (lambda () colour)) 'after colour))\n(newline)"
     :base "dynamic-wind guarantees the after-thunk on every way out of the body."
     :emergent "eng: borrow-and-return — the brush comes back even though the body failed, so the held list is empty afterwards. math: a transparent wrapper that adds a bracket and changes nothing observable, which is what lets it be dropped into a map. cs: with-restore, the general save-and-put-back, parameterised over how to read and write the thing."
     :meta "The third composition is the one to generalise from: capture the old value BEFORE the wind, set the new one in the before-thunk, put the old one back in the after-thunk. Every 'temporarily change X' in the language has this shape — and parameterize is exactly this construction packaged as syntax, with a parameter object as the X."
    )
    (:eng "The failure case is the only case worth testing here; the success case would work without the wind." :math "A wrapper that preserves the value composes with anything, which is why the map is unremarkable — and that is the point." :cs "with-restore is parameterize written by hand. Seeing that once demystifies the special form."))
)
